  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Through tonight, a shortwave trough could spark \nanother round of showers and storms, but it will be working against \na relatively stable boundary layer.\n\nAn additional round of severe thunderstorms is possible tonight. \nModel guidance has struggled with the convection today with \nuncertainty continuing into tonight. Currently, it looks as though \nthe backdoor cold front moves back through the area as a warm front \nlate this evening into tonight. Confidence in details regarding this \nremains relatively low, but should the region experience more \ndestabilization and erosion of the marine layer than is currently \nanticipated, storms will be capable of damaging winds, some hail, \nand perhaps an isolated tornado. The greater risk of any severe \nstorms will be toward our southern and western zones. As of right \nnow, model soundings continue to show a step inversion into tonight \nwith instability remaining elevated. However, forcing and shear \nvalues remain favorable. Locally heavy rainfall will also be a \nthreat with any training showers and storms, even if a severe \nthunderstorm threat does not materialize. Portions of eastern PA \nhave been highlighted in a Slight (level 2 out of 4) Risk for \nexcessive rainfall, which could lead to localized flash flooding.\n\nSPC maintains a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms for much of our area, but again, this risk is highly \nconditional and low confidence into tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across the \nregion on Thursday. \n\nFollowing the brief cool down in the marine layer today for most \nareas, hot and humid conditions are once again expected across the \nregion on Thursday as the frontal boundary lifts north again. With \nthe front to the north, high temperatures are forecast to climb into \nthe upper 80s to mid 90s for many areas (low to mid 80s far north).\nThese temperatures combined with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s \nwill again result in heat indices well into the 90s to low 100s \nsouth of I-78, perhaps even as high as 105 degrees for Delmarva.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of southeastern PA \nand NJ. This Advisory has been expanded into Delmarva given the \ncontinued trend of forecast heat indices near 105 degrees. North of \nthe current advisory area, maximum heat indices are forecast to top \nout in the low to mid 90s, thus the advisory is unlikely to need to \nbe expanded farther north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled conditions will continue on Thursday with \nthe potential for severe thunderstorms. Scattered thunderstorms are \nagain possible on Friday, mainly south of Philadelphia.\n\nAnother round of severe thunderstorms are possible on Thursday.\nFriday could also experience a few severe storms across Delmarva, \ndepending on how quickly the frontal boundary pushes south.\n\nSPC has a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe thunderstorms on \nThursday for Delmarva and a Marginal Risk (level 1) to as far north \nas the I-78 corridor. \n\nThe northwest flow pattern will remain in place on Thursday and will \ncontinue to be supportive of all severe threats, including damaging \nwinds, large hail, and isolated tornadoes, depending on how all the \nmesoscale details pan out. The threat for flash flooding is \ntypically lower with these patterns, but not zero. Training can \nstill occur and a single locale could have multiple days with heavy \nrainfall.\n\nFriday's SPC outlook only has a small portion of the Maryland\nEastern Shore and southern Delaware in a Marginal risk for \nsevere weather. Th rest of the area includes general \nthunderstorms (no severe risk), though there is still some \nuncertainty with this. Our latest forecast has about a 40-60% \nchance of showers and thunderstorms from about I-78 on \nsouthwards, including the entire Philadelphia metro area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry \nweather with at or below normal temperatures and lower humidity.\n\nBy Saturday, current model consensus indicates the frontal \nboundary will be pushing well south of our region, with \nCanadian high pressure building across the Northeast and Mid-\nAtlantic. This will largely end any shower and thunderstorm \npotential. Thus, Labor Day Weekend is still shaping up to \nfeature nice weather, with temperatures trending at or slightly \nbelow normal and lower humidity. The only road bump at the \nmoment, could be a cold front that approaches the forecast area \non Tuesday.",
